1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,968
This is not an animation.

2
00:00:01,968 --> 00:00:09,162
A real camera is flying through the most expensive machine human beings have ever built, two hundred fifty miles above your head.

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:14,979
It has been lived in, without a single day's break, for over twenty five years.

4
00:00:14,979 --> 00:00:20,906
Every person on Earth younger than that has never known a day with all of humanity on the planet.

5
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:25,437
Nobody can tell you what it actually cost. Not even NASA.

6
00:00:25,437 --> 00:00:30,079
The honest answers range from seventy five billion dollars, to a quarter of a trillion.

7
00:00:31,199 --> 00:00:34,787
And its builders have already signed the contract to destroy it.

8
00:00:34,787 --> 00:00:40,656
In January of 2031, all four hundred thirty tons of it will be steered into the Pacific Ocean.

9
00:00:40,656 --> 00:00:41,308
On purpose.

10
00:00:42,433 --> 00:00:48,168
This is the real story of the International Space Station, told with real footage, inside and out.

11
00:00:48,168 --> 00:00:54,240
Every number in this video is sourced, and where the official numbers disagree, we'll show you that too.

12
00:00:55,366 --> 00:01:00,617
Start with the fact almost nobody remembers. This thing very nearly never existed.

13
00:01:01,766 --> 00:01:02,630
January 1984.

14
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:10,407
President Reagan stands before Congress and directs NASA to build a permanently crewed space station, within a decade.

15
00:01:10,407 --> 00:01:12,999
The promised price: eight billion dollars.

16
00:01:14,133 --> 00:01:19,176
Nine years later, the program had spent over eleven billion dollars, and launched nothing.

17
00:01:19,176 --> 00:01:20,977
Not one piece of hardware.

18
00:01:22,099 --> 00:01:27,482
So in June of 1993, the House of Representatives voted on whether to kill it.

19
00:01:27,482 --> 00:01:32,147
The station survived by one vote. Two hundred sixteen, to two hundred fifteen.

20
00:01:32,147 --> 00:01:35,735
It would survive twenty two separate termination votes in total.

21
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:40,661
What saved it for good was the end of the Cold War.

22
00:01:40,661 --> 00:01:45,722
That same year, the White House merged the American station with Russia's planned successor to Mir.

23
00:01:45,722 --> 00:01:54,577
The station's Russian backbone, the module that still holds its main engines today, has a hull that was fabricated in the Soviet Union, in the mid nineteen eighties.

24
00:01:55,699 --> 00:01:59,252
No factory on Earth could assemble it. So they assembled it in orbit.

25
00:02:00,399 --> 00:02:01,643
November 20, 1998.

26
00:02:01,643 --> 00:02:07,032
A Russian rocket lifts the first module, Zarya, off the pad at Baikonur.

27
00:02:07,032 --> 00:02:14,494
Here's the twist almost every documentary gets wrong: Zarya was built in Moscow, but NASA paid for it.

28
00:02:14,494 --> 00:02:19,468
The first, Russian built piece of the station is legally American property.

29
00:02:20,599 --> 00:02:30,452
Two weeks later, the shuttle Endeavour brought up the first American module, Unity, and an astronaut named Nancy Currie reached out with the robot arm and caught Zarya.

30
00:02:30,452 --> 00:02:32,563
Three spacewalks wired the two together.

31
00:02:33,699 --> 00:02:41,808
When the hatches opened, commander Bob Cabana and cosmonaut Sergei Krikalev entered exactly side by side, so that neither nation could claim it entered first.

32
00:02:41,808 --> 00:02:49,269
Cabana's own words: if we were an international crew on an international space station, we needed to enter it as an international crew.

33
00:02:50,399 --> 00:02:53,942
On November 2nd, 2000, the first crew moved in.

34
00:02:53,942 --> 00:02:59,059
Commander Bill Shepherd, a navy seal, with cosmonauts Yuri Gidzenko and Sergei Krikalev.

35
00:02:59,059 --> 00:03:03,389
Krikalev floated in first, turning on the lights as he went.

36
00:03:03,389 --> 00:03:07,718
Their first tasks: get the hot water working, and the toilet.

37
00:03:08,866 --> 00:03:11,008
Shepherd kept a ship's log, navy style.

38
00:03:11,008 --> 00:03:15,292
The first entry ends with one line: we have all written some space history.

39
00:03:16,433 --> 00:03:22,673
Then, in February 2003, Columbia broke apart coming home, and assembly froze for almost four years.

40
00:03:22,673 --> 00:03:27,744
The station survived on skeleton crews of two, supplied only by Russian capsules.

41
00:03:28,866 --> 00:03:33,440
When the shuttles returned, the pace became relentless. The European lab.

42
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:38,846
The Japanese lab, so big it took three launches. The seven windowed cupola.

43
00:03:38,846 --> 00:03:44,252
By July 2011, after thirty seven shuttle flights, the American side was complete.

44
00:03:45,399 --> 00:03:47,879
It nearly came apart more than once.

45
00:03:47,879 --> 00:03:52,839
In 2007, a solar wing tore while unfurling, live, with the whole array energized.

46
00:03:52,839 --> 00:03:59,570
Astronaut Scott Parazynski rode a ninety foot boom to the tear and stitched it shut with improvised wire cufflinks.

47
00:03:59,570 --> 00:04:05,592
We told his story in our last video. This is what it looked like from his helmet.

48
00:04:06,733 --> 00:04:11,409
So what did all of that actually cost? Here is where it gets genuinely strange.

49
00:04:12,533 --> 00:04:23,645
The hundred and fifty billion dollar figure in this video's title, the one Guinness used to call this the most expensive object ever built, traces back to a single analysis, published in 2010.

50
00:04:23,645 --> 00:04:31,054
It added NASA's station budgets, to the partners' spending, to thirty six shuttle flights, priced at one point four billion dollars each.

51
00:04:32,199 --> 00:04:38,537
NASA's own inspector general audited the American share a few years later, and got seventy five billion through 2013.

52
00:04:38,537 --> 00:04:45,209
The entire gap between the two numbers comes down to one accounting choice: what you decide a shuttle flight costs.

53
00:04:46,333 --> 00:04:56,040
And astronaut Terry Virts, who commanded the station, told Time magazine the true lifetime total is probably over two hundred billion, maybe two hundred and fifty.

54
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:00,521
From Reagan's eight billion dollar promise, to a quarter of a trillion.

55
00:05:00,521 --> 00:05:01,641
Nobody actually knows.

56
00:05:02,766 --> 00:05:06,830
What we do know: keeping it flying costs three to four billion dollars every year.

57
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:12,248
Divide it out, and a single day of one astronaut's time comes to roughly seven and a half million dollars.

58
00:05:13,366 --> 00:05:17,222
Now step inside. Because the station's real product isn't science.

59
00:05:17,222 --> 00:05:23,005
It's seven people, kept alive, by a machine that never stops fighting to kill them.

60
00:05:24,133 --> 00:05:31,911
The living space is bigger than a six bedroom house, with two bathrooms, a gym, and a three hundred sixty degree bay window.

61
00:05:31,911 --> 00:05:36,646
It circles the Earth every ninety minutes. The crew watches sixteen sunrises a day.

62
00:05:37,766 --> 00:05:42,743
The air they breathe is made by splitting recycled water into oxygen and hydrogen.

63
00:05:42,743 --> 00:05:49,142
When that machine fails, and the Russian one has failed repeatedly, the backup is burning chemical oxygen candles.

64
00:05:49,142 --> 00:05:54,119
The same type of device that once set the space station Mir on fire.

65
00:05:55,266 --> 00:06:01,285
Even working perfectly, the air holds roughly ten times the carbon dioxide you're breathing right now.

66
00:06:01,285 --> 00:06:06,927
Astronaut Scott Kelly wrote that he could feel the levels change in his own body.

67
00:06:06,927 --> 00:06:08,432
Headaches. Burning eyes. Irritability.

68
00:06:09,566 --> 00:06:12,784
The water is more impressive, and more unsettling.

69
00:06:12,784 --> 00:06:17,610
Since 2023, the station recovers ninety eight percent of all its water.

70
00:06:17,610 --> 00:06:26,860
Urine, sweat, even the moisture in every breath, distilled back into drinking water that NASA's engineers say is cleaner than city tap water.

71
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:30,882
The crew has a saying: yesterday's coffee is tomorrow's coffee.

72
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,702
Power comes from an acre of solar panels.

73
00:06:34,702 --> 00:06:42,808
But for a third of every orbit, the Earth blocks the sun, so the whole station runs on batteries, through sixteen nights a day.

74
00:06:42,808 --> 00:06:46,523
Replacing those batteries took astronauts fourteen spacewalks, spread over four years.

75
00:06:47,666 --> 00:06:49,049
And the old batteries?

76
00:06:49,049 --> 00:06:54,927
In 2021, NASA threw a two and a half ton pallet of them overboard, to burn up.

77
00:06:54,927 --> 00:07:03,570
In March 2024, a piece the size of a soda can survived reentry, and punched through the roof of a family home in Naples, Florida.

78
00:07:03,570 --> 00:07:05,299
NASA confirmed it was theirs.

79
00:07:06,433 --> 00:07:09,846
The station's cooling runs on ammonia, which is toxic.

80
00:07:09,846 --> 00:07:14,775
In 2013, the crew watched frozen ammonia flakes drifting away from a leak.

81
00:07:14,775 --> 00:07:19,704
And one morning in 2015, the alarms said ammonia was inside the cabin.

82
00:07:19,704 --> 00:07:25,392
The crew masked up, abandoned the entire American half, and sealed the hatch behind them.

83
00:07:25,392 --> 00:07:30,701
It turned out to be a false alarm. For several hours, nobody knew that.

84
00:07:31,833 --> 00:07:34,524
Daily life aboard is stranger than the hardware.

85
00:07:35,666 --> 00:07:38,447
Astronauts sleep strapped inside phone booth sized cabins.

86
00:07:38,447 --> 00:07:42,967
In weightlessness, the carbon dioxide you exhale doesn't rise away from your face.

87
00:07:42,967 --> 00:07:45,053
It hovers there, in a bubble.

88
00:07:45,053 --> 00:07:50,268
Without a fan blowing on you all night, you wake up gasping, with a headache.

89
00:07:51,399 --> 00:07:54,273
The newest toilet cost twenty three million dollars.

90
00:07:54,273 --> 00:08:02,534
It works by airflow, has a three d printed titanium fan separator, and was the first designed from the start around female astronauts.

91
00:08:02,534 --> 00:08:05,766
Everything it collects feeds the water system. Tomorrow's coffee.

92
00:08:06,899 --> 00:08:14,753
Every crew member exercises about two hours a day, six days a week, because in weightlessness the body simply stops building itself.

93
00:08:14,753 --> 00:08:21,893
NASA's own data measured bone density losses of two to nearly four percent on long missions, even with the machines.

94
00:08:23,033 --> 00:08:26,091
There is no bread, only tortillas. Crumbs float into equipment.

95
00:08:26,091 --> 00:08:27,926
There is no laundry at all.

96
00:08:27,926 --> 00:08:33,432
Clothes are worn for days or weeks, then bagged as trash and burned up inside departing cargo ships.

97
00:08:33,432 --> 00:08:37,714
A crew of six throws away roughly nine hundred pounds of clothing a year.

98
00:08:38,833 --> 00:08:40,998
And space itself has a smell.

99
00:08:40,998 --> 00:08:46,771
Astronauts coming in from spacewalks describe it the same way: hot metal, welding fumes, burnt gunpowder.

100
00:08:46,771 --> 00:08:50,379
It clings to their suits and gloves in the airlock.

101
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:55,857
Twenty five years of continuous occupation means twenty five years of near misses.

102
00:08:55,857 --> 00:08:57,533
These are the real ones.

103
00:08:58,666 --> 00:08:59,368
August 2018.

104
00:08:59,368 --> 00:09:07,091
Pressure starts bleeding out of the station, and the crew traces it to a two millimeter hole in a docked Soyuz capsule.

105
00:09:07,091 --> 00:09:09,899
A cosmonaut plugs it with epoxy soaked gauze.

106
00:09:09,899 --> 00:09:14,462
Then the investigation finds something disturbing: the hole was made by a drill.

107
00:09:14,462 --> 00:09:15,515
From the inside.

108
00:09:16,633 --> 00:09:24,756
That December, two cosmonauts took knives to the capsule's hull insulation, live on NASA television, to sample the hole from outside.

109
00:09:24,756 --> 00:09:27,850
Russia finished its investigation, and never published it.

110
00:09:27,850 --> 00:09:30,558
Russian officials floated blaming an American astronaut.

111
00:09:30,558 --> 00:09:36,747
NASA called the suggestion baseless, and to this day, no cause has ever been made public.

112
00:09:37,866 --> 00:09:38,603
July 2021.

113
00:09:38,603 --> 00:09:43,026
A newly docked Russian lab module suddenly starts firing its thrusters, uncommanded.

114
00:09:43,026 --> 00:09:48,186
The station is dragged through a tumble. NASA said forty five degrees that day.

115
00:09:48,186 --> 00:09:54,820
The flight director later admitted it went around one and a half full times, and ended upside down.

116
00:09:54,820 --> 00:09:58,137
His words: this is no kidding. A real event.

117
00:09:59,266 --> 00:10:00,049
November 2021.

118
00:10:00,049 --> 00:10:09,049
Russia destroys one of its own dead satellites with a missile, fifteen hundred pieces of trackable debris, in an orbit crossing the station's.

119
00:10:09,049 --> 00:10:15,702
The seven people aboard, including two Russians, are woken and told to shelter inside their return capsules.

120
00:10:15,702 --> 00:10:19,615
The debris field came back around every ninety three minutes.

121
00:10:20,733 --> 00:10:24,952
And sometimes the near miss is quieter. Frank Rubio launched for six months.

122
00:10:24,952 --> 00:10:30,145
His ride home sprang a coolant leak while docked, and was declared unsafe to carry him.

123
00:10:30,145 --> 00:10:35,013
He came home after three hundred and seventy one days, the longest American spaceflight ever.

124
00:10:35,013 --> 00:10:39,881
His own words: if they had asked me up front, I probably would have declined.

125
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,028
Today, the quietest emergency of all is structural.

126
00:10:44,028 --> 00:10:53,114
The tunnel on the Russian segment, the same tunnel the first crew floated through in 2000, has been cracking and leaking air since 2019.

127
00:10:53,114 --> 00:10:57,278
NASA ranked it the single highest risk on the entire station.

128
00:10:57,278 --> 00:11:02,578
Russia says the welds are fatigued but safe. NASA has never accepted a cause.

129
00:11:02,578 --> 00:11:05,606
This year, Russia sealed the tunnel's hatch, permanently.

130
00:11:06,733 --> 00:11:10,704
A quarter of a trillion dollars. So what did humanity get?

131
00:11:11,833 --> 00:11:15,046
The critics have been brutal, and they weren't cranks.

132
00:11:15,046 --> 00:11:18,973
Nobel laureates dismissed its science. Britain's Astronomer Royal called it meagre.

133
00:11:18,973 --> 00:11:26,827
And NASA's own numbers gave them ammunition: at the end of 2013, about fifteen percent of the American experiment racks sat empty.

134
00:11:27,966 --> 00:11:33,700
But in July 2025, researchers finally did what nobody had done: they tested it.

135
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:40,254
They compared over thirteen hundred station experiments against matched work by the same scientists on Earth.

136
00:11:40,254 --> 00:11:43,940
Public station research was cited sixty three percent more.

137
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:46,397
Its patents, over eighty percent more.

138
00:11:46,397 --> 00:11:49,674
The station's science, it turns out, measurably outperforms.

139
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:53,621
Some of it could not have happened anywhere else.

140
00:11:53,621 --> 00:11:57,382
The coldest experiment in the known universe, watching atoms behave as waves.

141
00:11:57,382 --> 00:12:02,398
The twin study, one identical twin in orbit for a year, his brother on the ground.

142
00:12:02,398 --> 00:12:07,727
Even the shelf life of a fabric softener in your laundry aisle was modeled with station physics.

143
00:12:08,866 --> 00:12:20,738
And honesty demands the other side: the two billion dollar particle detector bolted to the truss has counted over two hundred fifty billion cosmic rays across fifteen years, and has not found the dark matter it hoped for.

144
00:12:20,738 --> 00:12:24,174
Its lead scientist's own summary: none of the results were predicted.

145
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:29,427
Which brings us to the ending. Because this machine now has an execution date.

146
00:12:30,566 --> 00:12:32,006
The station is old.

147
00:12:32,006 --> 00:12:37,044
By 2024, NASA counted five hundred eighty eight parts operating beyond their planned lifetimes.

148
00:12:37,044 --> 00:12:40,282
Boosting it to a graveyard orbit is physically impractical.

149
00:12:40,282 --> 00:12:43,881
Taking it apart is impossible, no vehicle exists that could.

150
00:12:43,881 --> 00:12:48,199
And nobody on Earth wants to buy it. That leaves one option.

151
00:12:49,333 --> 00:12:57,103
NASA has paid SpaceX up to eight hundred forty three million dollars to build the deorbit vehicle, a monster Dragon capsule with forty six thrusters.

152
00:12:57,103 --> 00:13:02,075
NASA itself puts the full cost of the funeral at around one and a half billion.

153
00:13:02,075 --> 00:13:04,562
The plan is public, and it is precise.

154
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:10,995
Through 2030, the station works normally. Then the boosts stop, and gravity takes over.

155
00:13:10,995 --> 00:13:19,695
The last crew rides it down to two hundred twenty kilometers, the lowest altitude their capsules are rated for, and then they leave.

156
00:13:19,695 --> 00:13:24,613
The last hatch closes, and humanity's longest continuous presence off the Earth, ends.

157
00:13:25,733 --> 00:13:33,511
In the final week, the deorbit vehicle fires for the last time over the emptiest stretch of ocean on Earth, Point Nemo.

158
00:13:33,511 --> 00:13:37,046
The station starts tearing apart around seventy eight kilometers up.

159
00:13:37,046 --> 00:13:39,521
Solar wings first, then the modules themselves.

160
00:13:39,521 --> 00:13:46,239
What survives, pieces from microwave sized to S U V sized, rains into a footprint two thousand kilometers long.

161
00:13:47,366 --> 00:13:49,406
Not everyone agrees on the timing.

162
00:13:49,406 --> 00:13:54,847
Elon Musk, whose company is building the deorbit vehicle, publicly recommended bringing it down years early.

163
00:13:54,847 --> 00:13:56,207
NASA held its date.

164
00:13:56,207 --> 00:14:04,367
And astronauts who lived aboard have said the quiet part: it is very sad, to spend this much and dump it in the ocean.

165
00:14:04,367 --> 00:14:08,447
But safety, as one analyst put it, is going to trump sentimentality.

166
00:14:09,566 --> 00:14:12,984
Here's the part that should worry you: the replacements aren't ready.

167
00:14:12,984 --> 00:14:15,780
The commercial stations meant to take over keep slipping.

168
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:25,722
Government auditors warn openly about a gap, years when the only station in orbit may be China's, which is doubling in size, and which American astronauts are banned by law from visiting.

169
00:14:25,722 --> 00:14:30,072
The country that was excluded from this station may end up owning the sky.

170
00:14:31,200 --> 00:14:35,421
The station turned twenty five last November. There was no party.

171
00:14:35,421 --> 00:14:42,328
The American government was shut down, NASA's press office was furloughed, and the milestone passed in near silence.

172
00:14:42,328 --> 00:14:47,317
Twenty five years of continuous human presence in space, and almost nobody noticed.

173
00:14:48,466 --> 00:14:56,742
Scott Kelly, who spent a year aboard, wrote that building it and keeping it alive is the hardest thing human beings have ever done.

174
00:14:56,742 --> 00:14:58,466
Not the rockets. The cooperation.

175
00:14:58,466 --> 00:15:05,707
Two former enemies, welding their hardware and their fates together for a quarter century, through every crisis on the ground below.

176
00:15:06,833 --> 00:15:14,717
Somewhere above you right now, someone is watching their sixteenth sunrise of the day through a window Europe traded for cargo space.

177
00:15:14,717 --> 00:15:18,301
They know the date the last hatch closes. January, 2031.

178
00:15:19,433 --> 00:15:21,688
The machine is only half the story.

179
00:15:21,688 --> 00:15:25,554
The people aboard have watched it flood, spin, and crack around them.

180
00:15:25,554 --> 00:15:30,065
Twelve astronauts told us, in their own words, what scared them most up there.

181
00:15:30,065 --> 00:15:32,320
That video is on your screen now.

182
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:35,864
Subscribe to mindwired, so you don't miss what we uncover next.

